<!-- ================= Developer Notes panel ================= -->
<div id="devNotesPanel" style="display:none">
    <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-header grey">
            <h2>Developer Notes</h2>
            <p>Technical notes for whoever maintains this tool.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body info-box">
            <h3>File / Runtime</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>This is a single .hta file (HTML Application), run via <code>mshta.exe</code>. It is NOT a normal web page and will not run in Chrome/Edge.</li>
                <li>All logic is VBScript inside the one &lt;script language="VBScript"&gt; block.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Outlook / Signature</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Emails are created via the Outlook COM object (<code>CreateObject("Outlook.Application")</code>), not mailto: links, because mailto: does not include the default signature.</li>
                <li><b>Signature comes from disk, not from Outlook's Object Model.</b> The "obvious" approach -- call <code>objMail.Display</code>, wait for the compose window to load, then read the now-signature-filled <code>objMail.HTMLBody</code> and prepend to it -- does NOT work under some organizations' policy: HTMLBody never picks up the signature no matter how long the script waits, so the draft goes out blank. <code>LoadSignature()</code> works around this entirely outside the Object Model: it reads the registry (<code>HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Office\&lt;ver&gt;\Common\MailSettings\NewSignature</code>) to find the default signature's name, then reads that signature's <code>.htm</code> file straight from <code>%APPDATA%\Microsoft\Signatures\</code>. <code>SendViaOutlook</code> builds the full body (<code>strBodyHtml & LoadSignature()</code>) and sets it BEFORE calling <code>Display()</code> -- there's no read-after-display race to manage, and no signature means <code>LoadSignature()</code> just returns "" and the email still sends fine, minus the signature.</li>
                <li><b>Blank body, second possible cause:</b> if the Outlook profile's default compose format is Plain Text or Rich Text (a common corporate/DLP policy setting), setting <code>.HTMLBody</code> has no visible effect -- Outlook keeps rendering the plain-text <code>Body</code>, and the draft looks completely empty even though the property "set" without error. <code>SendViaOutlook</code> explicitly sets <code>objMail.BodyFormat = 2</code> (olFormatHTML) right after <code>CreateItem</code> so HTML is guaranteed to be what actually renders, regardless of the mailbox's default format.</li>
                <li><b>Do NOT call <code>objMail.Recipients.ResolveAll</code> (breaks the whole app):</b> it can trigger Outlook's "Object Model Guard" security prompt (a program is trying to access address information...). If that dialog opens off-screen or behind another window, the synchronous COM call blocks forever, which freezes the entire single-threaded HTA -- not just email sending, EVERYTHING, including switching tabs, since nothing else can run while the call is stuck waiting on a hidden dialog. To/CC still resolve fine on their own (same as always) once the user clicks into the field or hits Send -- there's no safe way to force that resolution early, so don't try.</li>
                <li>Emails open as drafts (<code>.Display</code>), not sent automatically, so a human reviews before sending. Swap to <code>.Send</code> only if that's intentional.</li>
                <li>Attachments use <code>objMail.Attachments.Add</code> with the path from a file &lt;input&gt;. HTA runs in the Local Machine security zone, so file inputs expose the real full path, unlike a normal browser's fake path.</li>
                <li>Since <code>Attachments.Add</code> only takes one path at a time, <code>SendViaOutlook</code> takes both a single <code>strAttachmentPath</code> (used by Category 2) and an <code>arrExtraAttachments</code> array (used by Tab 2's "Add Attachments" queue, see <code>gTab2Attachments</code> / <code>AddTab2Attachment</code> / <code>GetTab2AttachmentArray</code>). Callers that don't need it pass <code>Array()</code>.</li>
                <li><b>Missing attachments now warn instead of vanishing silently (fixed 2026-09-11).</b> If a file selected earlier gets moved, renamed, or deleted before the draft is built, <code>SendViaOutlook</code> used to just skip it via <code>FileExists</code> with no feedback -- the user believed it was attached when it wasn't. It now collects every missing path into <code>missingAttachments</code> and shows one <code>MsgBox</code> naming them (still before <code>Display()</code>) before opening the draft without them.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Reliability fixes (2026-09-11)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>User-typed/pasted text is now HTML-escaped before it goes into an email body.</b> <code>EscapeHtml()</code> (escapes <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>) is applied to every free-text field value spliced into a <code>strBodyHtml</code> string in <code>ValidateAndSend1</code>, <code>ValidateAndSend2</code>, and <code>GenerateSeverityEmail</code>. Before this, a pasted error message or log snippet containing angle brackets or an ampersand (stack traces, generics like <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code>, XML/HTML fragments -- all common in support tickets) got interpreted as real markup by Outlook's renderer and could silently eat or mangle part of the email, with nothing visibly wrong until the recipient read a garbled message.</li>
                <li><b>Line breaks in pasted text are now preserved reliably.</b> <code>NlToBr()</code> normalizes CRLF, lone LF, and lone CR to <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> before the text is inserted. The old code only handled CRLF (<code>Replace(x, vbCrLf, "&lt;br&gt;")</code>), so text pasted from sources that use bare <code>\n</code> (common from web textareas/non-Windows tools) lost its line breaks and ran together as one paragraph. Apply <code>EscapeHtml()</code> first, then <code>NlToBr()</code> on the result -- reversing the order would escape the <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> tags this function inserts.</li>
                <li>Both helpers live near the top of the script, right after the <code>gLogFilePath</code>/<code>gFso</code> declarations, alongside <code>CsvEscapeField</code>/<code>ParseCsvLine</code> (see Log section above).</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Deferred send / "please wait" indicator (fixed 2026-09-11)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>The problem:</b> <code>CreateObject("Outlook.Application")</code> launches Outlook if it isn't already running, which can take several seconds. Because HTA/VBScript is single-threaded, the ENTIRE UI -- not just the button clicked -- was unresponsive that whole time with zero visual feedback, which reads as a crash to a normal user (especially first thing in the morning before anyone's opened Outlook yet).</li>
                <li><b>Why a simple "please wait" message didn't just work:</b> setting a status message and cursor immediately before the blocking call does NOT actually paint on screen first -- old IE's rendering engine queues the repaint but doesn't flush it until the current script yields back to the browser's message loop. Since the blocking <code>CreateObject</code>/<code>Display</code> call happens in the same script tick, the message would never actually be visible until AFTER the freeze was already over.</li>
                <li><b>The fix:</b> <code>ValidateAndSend1</code>, <code>ValidateAndSend2</code>, and <code>GenerateSeverityEmail</code> now build the subject/body/recipients as before, then call <code>BeginSend(kind, statusID, ...)</code> instead of <code>SendViaOutlook(...)</code> directly. <code>BeginSend</code> stashes everything needed into module-level <code>gPending*</code> globals, sets the wait cursor + "Opening Outlook -- please wait..." status text, and defers the actual send via <code>window.setTimeout "FinishSend", 30</code>. Returning from <code>BeginSend</code> lets the engine paint before the timer fires 30ms later, so the message is genuinely visible during the freeze instead of only after it. <code>FinishSend</code> calls <code>SendViaOutlook</code> with the stashed args, then does the per-tab success/failure cleanup (logging, clearing fields, resetting the status text) via a <code>Select Case gPendingKind</code> ("tab1" / "tab2" / "sev", using <code>gPendingSevIdx</code> for which severity tab).</li>
                <li><b>gSendInProgress guard:</b> the 30ms deferral window is enough time for a user to click a second Generate/Send button before the first send finishes, which would overwrite the <code>gPending*</code> globals out from under the in-flight send. <code>BeginSend</code> exits immediately if <code>gSendInProgress</code> is already <code>True</code>; <code>FinishSend</code> clears it once the send completes. This didn't exist before because the old code was fully synchronous with no such window.</li>
                <li>This still doesn't make the Outlook launch itself non-blocking -- there's no real threading available in VBScript/HTA -- it just makes the wait visible and explained instead of looking like a freeze.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Rendering quirks (mshta / old IE engine)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>No DOCTYPE is used, on purpose. Adding one to force a modern IE document mode broke VBScript's inline onclick handler binding when tested. Keep this file without a DOCTYPE.</li>
                <li>Because of that, compound CSS class selectors (e.g. .tab-btn-red.active) are unreliable. Active/inactive tab colors are set directly via inline style in VBScript instead (see <code>SetTabButtonState</code> / <code>SetCategoryButtonState</code>), not by toggling CSS classes.</li>
                <li>&lt;select&gt; elements ignore width:100% and size to their widest &lt;option&gt; text in this engine -- keep option label text short.</li>
                <li>Dynamically building table rows with <code>document.createElement("tr")</code> + innerHTML does not work reliably here. Use <code>tbody.insertRow(-1)</code> / <code>row.insertCell(-1)</code> instead (see <code>AddLogRow</code>).</li>
                <li>A fixed-position overlay (<code>position:fixed</code>) does not render reliably either -- that's why this panel and How To are shown/hidden in-place via <code>HideAllPanels</code> / <code>ShowPanel</code>, the same mechanism as every other tab, instead of a floating modal.</li>
                <li>VBScript's "On Error Resume Next" only suppresses errors in the procedure it's declared in -- it does not propagate into Subs/Functions it calls. Any Sub that touches the DOM should have its own On Error Resume Next, or one unhandled error partway through can leave the UI in a broken state (e.g. two panels visible at once).</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Config you'll likely need to edit</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Agent list (9 agents) -- Tab 1 form, the &lt;select id="ctrlAgent"&gt; markup.</li>
                <li><code>Tab2ToEmails</code> / <code>Tab2CcEmails</code> -- top of the script.</li>
                <li><code>SevTabName()</code> / <code>SevTabDesc()</code> / <code>SevTabEmail()</code> / <code>SevTabIntro()</code> -- top of the script, one entry per Category 2 tab (1-5).</li>
                <li><code>DevNotesPassword</code> -- top of the script (see Security note below).</li>
                <li><b>Every visible title, tab label, and description in this file is a placeholder using a unique <code>{{TOKEN}}</code> name (changed 2026-09-11)</b> -- e.g. <code>{{APP_TITLE}}</code>, <code>{{TAB1_NAME}}</code>, <code>{{SEV3_PANEL_DESC}}</code>. Before this, every one of these placeholders shared the exact same literal text ("Enter Your Title Here" / "Enter Your Tab N Here" / "Enter your description here.") across many unrelated spots -- a global Find & Replace on that text couldn't tell the app's main title apart from a severity tab's panel title apart from another severity tab's panel title, since they were all byte-identical. Each <code>{{TOKEN}}</code> is now unique to what it represents, and appears in EVERY place that value needs to show up consistently (e.g. <code>{{SEV1_NAME}}</code> appears in the nav button, the <code>SevTabName(1)</code> script config, and the How-To text) -- so a single Find & Replace per token updates it everywhere at once, correctly. Search the file for <code>{{</code> to find every remaining placeholder.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Log</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Tab 1's Agent Log is persisted to <code>EmailLog.csv</code> next to this .hta (LogID, TicketNumber, AgentName, AgentEmail, Timestamp) and reloaded into the table on startup via <code>InitLog</code>.</li>
                <li><b>CSV fields are properly quoted (fixed 2026-09-11).</b> Ticket numbers/agent names are free-typed text and can contain a comma (e.g. a pasted "1,234") or a quote -- writing/reading them with a plain <code>&amp;</code>-join and <code>Split(line, ",")</code> silently shifted every column after the first comma, corrupting that row permanently. <code>CsvEscapeField()</code> (write) and <code>ParseCsvLine()</code> (read) now do minimal RFC-4180-style quoting -- a field is wrapped in quotes (with internal quotes doubled) only if it contains a comma, quote, or line break. Any old rows already written by the previous unquoted version are NOT retroactively fixed and may still parse wrong.</li>
                <li><b>Startup only renders today's rows, safety-capped at <code>MAX_LOG_ROWS_SHOWN</code> (200) (changed 2026-09-11).</b> <code>InitLog</code> still reads the entire file every launch to compute the next LogID correctly (across all days, so numbering never resets), but only calls <code>AddLogRow</code> (a DOM <code>insertRow</code>) for rows whose Timestamp field is today's date (<code>DateValue(parts(4)) = Date</code>) -- yesterday's and older entries would otherwise just be clutter for "who's been assigned what so far today." <code>MAX_LOG_ROWS_SHOWN</code> is now just a safety cap on top of that (in case one day somehow has 200+ urgents), not the primary limit. The full history for every day still lives in <code>EmailLog.csv</code> itself (open it in Excel to see everything); only the on-screen table is filtered.</li>
                <li><b>Table sorts newest-first (changed 2026-09-11).</b> <code>AddLogRow</code> now does <code>tbody.insertRow(0)</code> instead of <code>insertRow(-1)</code>, so the most recently assigned ticket is always the top row -- both for today's history replayed at startup (each older row gets pushed down as a newer one is inserted above it, since the replay itself is still oldest-to-newest) and for a brand-new entry logged live during the session.</li>
                <li>The table now has a <b>Timestamp</b> column (5th cell) -- <code>AddLogRow</code> takes a 5th <code>ts</code> parameter and <code>LogEmail</code>/<code>InitLog</code> both pass it through from the CSV's existing Timestamp field (it was already being written to disk, just never rendered on screen before).</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Pass Down Info (added 2026-09-11)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>The <b>Pass Down Info</b> button (next to Generate Email, Tab 1) calls <code>GeneratePassDown</code>, which builds a shift-handoff summary and copies it to the clipboard via <code>window.clipboardData.setData "Text", txt</code> -- the native IE/mshta clipboard API (no ActiveX prompt, works directly in the Local Machine zone this app runs in). Paste it into Teams, email, or anywhere else.</li>
                <li>It first shows an <code>InputBox</code> for an optional free-text note (Cancel or leaving it blank is fine -- the note section is just skipped).</li>
                <li>It then reads the first <code>Min(5, actual row count)</code> rows directly out of <code>tbody#logBody</code> -- NOT by re-reading <code>EmailLog.csv</code> -- since the table is already the live, today-only, newest-first view on screen. Its first 5 rows are, by construction, "the last 5 assigned urgent tickets," because <code>AddLogRow</code> inserts every new row at the top (see Log section above). Each line includes the ticket number, agent name, agent email, and timestamp (cells 1-4; cell 0/LogID is left out of the copied text as internal bookkeeping, not something another agent needs).</li>
                <li>If the table is empty (no urgents assigned yet today), the copied text says so instead of an empty list -- it still copies something reasonable rather than silently producing a broken/blank pass-down message.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Security note</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>The Developer Notes password is a basic deterrent only, not real security. This file is plain text, so anyone can open it in Notepad and read the password, or just delete the check. Don't put anything truly sensitive in this file.</li>
            </ul>

            <button class="close-btn" onclick="CloseDevNotes">Close</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

